Round 32 is done and live. The TLDR: the steel fleet no longer wears one Victorian paint scheme from 1858 to 2017 — fastening, bottom colour and cove stripe are now era facts driven by the data — and the site deployed, ending a three-round outage backlog.

**What changed**

- **Era-keyed steel dress, as a class** (queue item 2). Each of the nine steel hulls now carries the year it is depicted at in `vessels.json`. From that, `hull.js` derives three new shader uniforms: welded vs riveted (welded from 1950), antifouling colour (Victorian salmon to 1890, red-brown oxide to 1955, modern oxide red after, with Yamato's dark IJN hull-red as a per-ship record), and the gilt cove line, which is now a documented per-ship field — Great Eastern, the steamer and Titanic keep theirs; no warship or modern ship has one. The welded branch of the shader draws flush hairline butts on a coarser plate grid, no rivets, and the "hungry horse" — shell dished between web frames at a real frame spacing.
- **A third instance of the same fault, found on the way**: the Shipwright's stage card told Titanic and Yamato the welded-block construction story. Steel before 1950 now gets a riveted-plating narrative — "three million rivets", not prefabricated blocks.
- **`build_data.py` is guarded** (queue item 1). It now refuses to overwrite a `vessels.json` that carries keys the generator doesn't produce — proven by running it: it refused, naming 27 hand-edited keys, and left the file untouched.

**Verification** — audit 25/25, with two new data rules (steel hull without a year; cove on a post-1950 hull), both proven live by breaking the data before proving them clean. The ratchet's control worked exactly as designed: **Great Eastern moved 0.000%**, so the refactor changed nothing the era rule says shouldn't change, while the six aimed frames moved 0.17–1.09% and were each classified and accepted. Three new per-ship baselines (Preussen, steamer, Dreadnought) mean every steel hull is now scored individually. Twelve-bearing spins confirmed the new dress from all angles.

**Deploy** — the push again created no workflow run and githubstatus still said `major_outage`, but a manual dispatch succeeded anyway: run 31128442199 completed and the live stamp moved to **1786052194**, this round's. That also carried round 31's stranded work live. The lesson went into HANDOFF: the status page is evidence about the platform, not about your run.

Next in the queue: the float datum (~2 m of antifouling showing above the waterline, fleet-wide — measure before tuning), then the Sea-view spot check of the remaining steel ships.
